   "response_text": "All 588 tests pass, nothing broke.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Cause:** In `src/click/parser.py`, the `OptionParser._process_opts` method split a long option on **every** equals sign and kept only the first two fragments:\n\n```python\nlong_opt, explicit_value = arg.split(\"=\")[:2]\n```\n\nSo `--name=a=b=c` \u2192 `[\"--name\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]` \u2192 `long_opt=\"--name\"`, `explicit_value=\"a\"` \u2014 everything after the second `=` was silently dropped.\n\n**Fix:** Split only on the first equals sign so the remainder is preserved verbatim:\n\n```python\nlong_opt, explicit_value = arg.split(\"=\", 1)\n```\n\nNow `--name=a=b=c` yields the value `a=b=c`, which is the documented/expected behavior (everything after the first `=` is the value). Note this also mattered for short/prefixed options matching long-option code paths, and values containing `=` (e.g. URLs/query strings) now survive intact.\n\n**Verification:** Confirmed with manual cases (`a=b=c`, empty value `--name=`, multiple options, values with `=` and `&`), and the full test suite passes (588 passed, 22 skipped, 1 xfailed). No test files were touched.\n\nDONE",
